  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Friday...\n\nAs a low deepens along the BC coast, a surge of hot & dry air from\nthe southwest will push temps over 100F today across the region. \nLofted smoke may have a slight impact, but 700mb temps of +17C to \n+20C should yield highs in the 102-107F range for many locations. \nDaily records are likely to be broken, and some August temperature\nrecords will be challenged.\n\nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect from noon to midnight. \nPlease protect yourself from the heat! Fire weather conditions\nwill be critical due to the heat and very low humidity (10% or\nless) and somewhat breezy S-SW winds over western areas. A period\nof lee side troffing (w/ very poor RH recovery) and an eventual \nweak frontal passage and shift to breezy NW-N winds late tonight \nwill extend the fire concerns across most of the area into Sunday.\nTomorrow will not be as hot but still quite dry. Please take care\nnot to spark a fire!\n\nA stronger cold front arrives Sunday night, setting the stage for\na much cooler Monday & Tuesday. Expect scattered showers (mainly\nwest & north) associated with mid level frontogenesis Sunday\nnight, and cannot rule out a few showers w/ the upper trof axis\nMonday. Either way, the trof will not dig far enough south to\nbring meaningful precip.\n\nSmoke impacts: Hazy skies due to lofted wildfire smoke will \ncontinue. It is possible that we see an increase in surface based \nsmoke per subsidence behind tonight's cold front, and especially \nbehind the stronger front Sunday night. This is of high \nuncertainty at this time and will need to be watched.\n\nAs already mentioned, a notable break from the heat (including\nmuch cooler nights) is on track for Monday & Tuesday. Look for\nhighs in the 70s to lower 80s on these days. There seems to be \nmodel uncertainty with regard to how long the flow stays \nnorthwesterly, but eventually we will transition to flat ridging \nand warmer temps through the 2nd half of the week. Look for 80s\nWednesday and 90s Thursday & Friday...though some ensembles show \nanother weak cold front at some point and this uncertainty is\nshown in the cluster analyses. Either way, the flattened flow will\nkeep monsoon moisture suppressed and greater plains moisture to\nour east and southeast. Thus a dry forecast for Tuesday thru\nFriday.\n\nBelow is a summary of Saturday forecast highs, with daily and \nAugust records, at each of our main climate stations.\n\nSITE      8/1 FORECAST   8/1 RECORD      AUG RECORD\nBillings     105         102 (2008)    105 (8/2/2024)\nLivingston \t 104 \t\t 101 (2000)    105 (8/5/1961) \nMiles City \t 105 \t\t 104 (2008)    110 (8/7/1995) \nSheridan \t 104\t\t 103 (2008)    106 (8/7/1983) \nBaker\t\t 101 \t\t 99  (2022)    108 (8/12/2018)\n\nJKL",
